short={'RASC-ME':'RASC-ME','Seasonal-ME':'Seasonal-ME','Ridge-ME':'Ridge-ME','LGB-Full-ME':'LightGBM full-ME','LGB-Recent-ME':'LightGBM recent-ME','LGB-NoState-ME':'LightGBM no-state-ME','GlobalCal-ME':'Global calibration-ME','Legacy-price':'Price-only market head','RASC-direct':'RASC direct','DA-persistent':'Published DA persistent','DA-myopic':'Published DA myopic','RT-lag':'Lagged RT','DAC':'Direct Action Controller'}

def write_main():
    doc=docnew();para(doc,TITLE,'Title');heading(doc,'Abstract',1)
    abstract=(f'Computing flexibility has economic value only when electricity responses preserve the work they defer. We integrate a trainable prediction and state-cost decision module into a frozen WorldMoE framework, with differentiable allocation during training and deterministic service constraints during execution. In a four-zone 2025 New York electricity-market replay, three optimization seeds save US${usd(mean)} on average ({100*mean/ref:.2f}% of earliest-service cost), with no substantive late or closing fluid work. This captures {capture:.2f}% of a certified US${usd(ceiling)} savings ceiling. Restricting pre-publication information {early_verb} savings by US${usd(abs(early))}, while a deadline-aware electrical guard exposes the cost of delivering flexibility under import and ramp limits. Paired calendar and event-influence analyses qualify the uncertainty and concentration of the gains. The framework connects forecasting, service commitments and physical delivery through a common work ledger and independently computed settlement.')
    para(doc,abstract)
    para(doc,'Computing facilities increasingly shape electricity demand and power-system planning.[[1,2,3]] Their flexibility lies partly in when admitted work is performed. Demand-response studies and recent field demonstrations establish that software can change data-centre power while preserving specified service outcomes.[[4,5]] The remaining operational question is how forecast information and deferred service obligations jointly determine economic value over continuous operation.')
    para(doc,'Carbon-aware capacity allocation, electricity-market formulations and probabilistic scheduling models connect computing demand to power-system conditions.[[6,7,8]] Workload characteristics determine the flexibility available at a given service cost.[[9]] A useful decision system must nevertheless distinguish a low forecast price from an admissible, physically economical execution slot. A slot can be cheap in price but expensive after cooling, photovoltaic supply, occupied capacity and pending deadlines are considered.')
    para(doc,'Electricity-price forecasting benchmarks stress comparable inputs and explicit evaluation rules.[[10,11]] Task-oriented learning offers a way to connect predictive representations to downstream operating objectives.[[12,13]] We use this connection within WorldMoE: market forecasts retain a separately scored, price-consistent output, while a joint module learns state-dependent electrical costs and values the use of scarce future service capacity. Work conservation, deadlines and settlement remain explicit constraints and accounts.')
    para(doc,'We evaluate the resulting framework through four complementary questions: how much saving the service contract can permit, when information becomes economically usable, whether electrical restrictions preserve service delivery, and how strongly annual differences depend on particular price events. These analyses use the final WorldMoE checkpoints. External frozen baselines compare complete operating pipelines under common demand, forcing and settlement; they do not identify the isolated effect of a particular neural component (Fig. 1).')
    figure(doc,'Fig1_WorldMoE_architecture','Fig. 1 | WorldMoE with a joint prediction and state-cost decision module. a, Frozen W0/W1 representations and cached R2 market features feed a trainable adapter and market–physics attention. Work-to-time attention conditions the cost decoder on known service obligations. The protected price branch applies an APIC-preserving shift before queue-conditioned decision processing. Training differentiates through 16-cohort constrained allocations; deployment executes only the current action and settles the resulting trajectory independently. The cross-moment decoder approximates price–PV cost dependence and is not a fully calibrated joint copula. b, Parameter fitting uses 2021–2023, checkpoint selection uses 2024, and frozen-model temporal evaluation uses 2025. Physical decision labels are available for 2022–2023.')
    heading(doc,'Results',1)
    heading(doc,'WorldMoE converts predictive information into completed service')
    para(doc,'The four-site portfolio represents WEST, CAPITL, N.Y.C. and LONGIL, with 6.556 MW of variable information-technology (IT) capacity, 0.923 MW of idle demand, 4 MW of PV capacity and an 8.601 MW import limit. Trace-driven Eagle arrivals require 2,981.607 MWh of annual variable IT service.[[14]] The mean variable-capacity utilization is 5.19%; concentrated arrivals, rather than mean utilization, determine service pressure. The complete 2025 local calendar contains 35,040 quarter-hours, and every arrival remains in the workload and account.')
    para(doc,f'The selected refinement epochs are {", ".join(str(x) for x in S["selected_epochs"])} for seeds {", ".join(str(x) for x in S["seeds"])}. They are chosen separately by eligible 2024 annual savings, with no 2025 weight updates or checkpoint reselection. In 2025, annual savings span US${usd(F.saving_usd.min())}–{usd(F.saving_usd.max())}, with a mean of US${usd(mean)} and a sample standard deviation of US${usd(F.saving_usd.std(ddof=1))}. Mean policy cost is US${usd(cost)} against the common US${usd(ref)} earliest-service reference. All three executions have late work below 10⁻⁸ IT MWh and zero closing work (Fig. 2; Supplementary Table 1).')
    para(doc,f'The runs shift {shift:,.1f} MWh of IT service on average. Service-adjusted daily-loss CVaR95 is US${usd(risk,2)}, and the maximum fleet recovery import is {S["metrics"]["max_fleet_recovery_mw"]["max"]:.3f} MW. These quantities describe the physical movement and downside accompanying the financial saving; annual value alone does not establish a favorable outcome on every operating dimension. The three seeds vary the optimization order and auxiliary training draws from a common pretrained initialization and frozen feature cache. Their range therefore measures refinement variability, not three independent backbone training runs.')
    figure(doc,'Fig2_WorldMoE_results','Fig. 2 | Completed-service economics and optimization variability. a, Full-year savings for seeds 20260920, 20260921 and 20260922 (suffixes shown), with each ratio to the same certified savings ceiling. The 80% line is a descriptive engineering target. b, Full-2024 annual economic evaluations every two refinement epochs; stars mark the eligible checkpoints restored for testing. Initial checkpoints are included in selection. c, Cumulative service-adjusted savings for WorldMoE and two external frozen pipelines on the same 2025 calendar. d, Empirical distributions of all 365 daily service-adjusted savings for each WorldMoE seed, shown on a symmetric-log horizontal scale with a US$50 linear region. Seed ranges and paired calendar intervals describe different uncertainties.')
    heading(doc,'External benchmarks and certified bounds delimit the economic opportunity')
    rasc=B[B.model.eq('RASC-ME')].iloc[0];season=B[B.model.eq('Seasonal-ME')].iloc[0]
    para(doc,f'Under the same 24 h service contract and electricity account, RASC-ME saves US${usd(rasc.saving)}, and Seasonal-ME saves US${usd(season.saving)}. The WorldMoE-minus-RASC-ME mean annual difference is US${usd(rasc_interval.difference_usd)}; its paired seven-day-block 95% interval is [US${usd(rasc_interval.lower95_usd)}, US${usd(rasc_interval.upper95_usd)}]. The benchmarks retain their own forecasting and decision mechanisms, so these differences evaluate complete pipelines. All service and closing-work outcomes remain visible alongside savings (Table 1).')
    score=FS.post_rt_pinball_usd_mwh.mean();rasc_score=pd.read_csv(D/'original/apic_and_forecast_controls.csv').set_index('model').loc['RASC-ME','pinball']
    relation='lower' if score<rasc_score else 'higher'
    para(doc,f'The queue-independent WorldMoE RT forecast has mean post-publication pinball loss {score:.3f} US$/MWh, {100*abs(score-rasc_score)/rasc_score:.2f}% {relation} than RASC-ME’s {rasc_score:.3f} US$/MWh. Its nominal 90% interval coverage is {FS.post_rt_coverage90_pct.mean():.2f}%. These scores evaluate the price output before the task-only cost bias and opportunity score. Price quality and operating value remain distinct measurements (Fig. 3d; Supplementary Table 2).')
    headers=['Pipeline','Saving\nUS$k','Saving\n%','Shifted IT\nMWh','CVaR95\nUS$','Late IT\nMWh','Closing IT\nMWh']
    rows=[['Earliest service','0.00','0.00','0.0','0.00','0.000','0.000'],['WorldMoE (3 seeds)',usd(mean/1000,2),perc(100*mean/ref),usd(shift,1),usd(risk,2),'0.000','0.000']]
    for r in B.sort_values('saving',ascending=False).itertuples():rows.append([short[r.model],usd(r.saving/1000,2),perc(100*r.saving/ref),usd(r.shifted,1),usd(r.cvar,2),usd(r.late,3),usd(r.closing,3)])
    table(doc,'Table 1 | Full-year 2025 operating benchmarks at 24 h. WorldMoE reports three-seed means; external baselines are frozen comparators. Savings and CVaR95 use the same service-adjusted account. All closing work is valued, including nonzero residuals in the price controls. “0.000” for WorldMoE lateness denotes values below 10⁻⁸ IT MWh.',headers,rows,[2.0,.95,.7,.95,.95,.73,.72])
    para(doc,'An earliest-service queue requires a minimum common window of 6.5 h at full capacity, increasing to 9.25 h at 75% capacity and 15 h at 50%. This is a workload-and-capacity property, independent of forecast accuracy. Economic bounds ask a different question: what savings remain possible after capacity, release times and deadline prefixes are imposed?')
    para(doc,f'A perfect-information convex relaxation with a separately evaluated Lagrangian certificate gives a 24 h cost lower bound of US${usd(ref-ceiling)} and therefore a savings upper bound of US${usd(ceiling)}. The 12 and 16 h ceilings are US$97,802 and US$113,310. WorldMoE’s mean saving is {capture:.2f}% of the 24 h ceiling (Fig. 3c). Because the ceiling can exceed the unknown attainable optimum, this ratio is a conservative lower fraction of optimal savings, not a measured exact optimality ratio. The bound is not an executable policy. Twelve workload–year–window certificates, covering Eagle and Frontier in 2024 and 2025, remain separately reported (Supplementary Table 3).')
    figure(doc,'Fig3_benchmarks_and_bounds','Fig. 3 | External operating benchmarks and independently certified headroom. a, Annual savings at 24 h for WorldMoE and thirteen external frozen pipelines; the WorldMoE whisker is its three-seed range. Table 1 reports outstanding and late work. b, Minimum feasible common windows for the unchanged Eagle arrival trace at eleven capacity fractions. c, Certified perfect-information savings ceilings at 12, 16 and 24 h, with the evaluated WorldMoE result shown only at 24 h. No unmeasured short-window WorldMoE result is implied. d, Post-publication RT pinball loss on the common calendar; WorldMoE excludes task-specific value adjustments from the price forecast. Economic comparisons in a are complete-pipeline benchmarks, not forecast-only ablations.')
    heading(doc,'Most value survives the restricted publication gate')
    para(doc,'For each final checkpoint, we remove neural market availability and associated issued physical-prior access before target-day DA publication. A training-period historical price fallback replaces the early market forecast; causal completed observations and seasonal physical fallback remain available. The post-publication forecast path, decision module, work arrivals, controller and financial account are unchanged. This intervention evaluates the complete early information pathway, including its associated state-cost availability; it does not isolate the WorldMoE backbone alone.')
    para(doc,f'The restricted pathway saves US${usd(I.post_only_usd.mean())} on average, retaining {100*I.post_only_usd.mean()/mean:.2f}% of native-pathway savings. Relative to native access, it {early_verb} annual savings by US${usd(abs(early))}; the native-minus-restricted seed range is [US${usd(I.early_path_usd.min())}, US${usd(I.early_path_usd.max())}]. The paired seven-day-block 95% interval for the mean increment is [US${usd(info_interval.lower95_usd)}, US${usd(info_interval.upper95_usd)}]. Monthly and continuous cumulative differences show when the increment is realized, while preserving the same full-year service trajectories (Fig. 4).')
    para(doc,'The intervention changes the information available when a decision is made. It does not reveal realized future prices or physics, remove difficult dates, refit any parameter or select a new checkpoint. Its result therefore belongs to the fixed final model under the specified publication restriction. Supplementary Tables 4 and 5 provide all seed-level values and paired intervals.')
    figure(doc,'Fig4_information_timing','Fig. 4 | Same-checkpoint value of the early information pathway. a, Native and post-publication-only annual savings for all three WorldMoE optimization seeds. b, Seed-level native-minus-restricted differences, together with a paired seven-day-block 95% interval computed from seed-mean daily differences using 2,000 resamples. This interval is not a confidence interval over three independent training lineages. c, Monthly increments on the unchanged 2025 calendar. d, Cumulative mean increments; shading is the range across three seeds. The intervention suppresses early neural and issued physical-prior access while preserving completed observations, post-publication forecasts and settlement.')
    heading(doc,'Electrical constraints expose the cost of service delivery')
    para(doc,'We project each final WorldMoE request stream through five conditions: 75% or 50% of the original site import limit, upward ramps of 25% or 12.5% of that limit per quarter-hour, and a combined 75% cap with a 25% ramp. Every projected reference serves the same workload through the same electrical restriction. Service deferred by the projection is retained in the queue. A deadline-prefix guard can advance already released work when the remaining conservative capacity would otherwise be insufficient.')
    para(doc,f'Under the 25% ramp condition (0.537556 MW per site per quarter-hour), deadline-aware execution saves US${usd(ramp.saving_usd)} against its constraint-matched reference and retains {ramp.savings_retained_pct:.2f}% of unconstrained savings. The maximum upward site ramp falls by {ramp_reduction:.2f}%. Projection alone leaves {projection.loc["ramp25","late_mean"]:.6f} IT MWh late on average; guarded execution leaves at most {ramp.late_max:.3g} IT MWh late across seeds. Across the five guarded conditions, {guard_zero} have maximum lateness below 10⁻⁸ IT MWh (Fig. 5; Supplementary Tables 6 and 7).')
    para(doc,f'The 25% ramp reference costs US${usd(ramp.reference_cost_usd,2)}, compared with US${usd(ref,2)} without the added restriction; the corresponding guarded policy costs US${usd(ramp.policy_cost_usd,2)}. Savings retention therefore compares two separately matched references. The measured ramp–value points are not an optimal constrained frontier. These tests project a frozen causal request stream and retain execution debt; they do not retrain the neural module or feed projected backlog into a newly optimized constrained controller.')
    figure(doc,'Fig5_electrical_delivery','Fig. 5 | Electrical delivery of the final WorldMoE request streams. a, Three-seed mean savings under five import/ramp conditions, comparing projection alone with deadline-aware projection. The dashed line is unconstrained WorldMoE savings; daggers mark projection-only cases with late service. b, Mean late IT work; all per-seed maxima and closing obligations are retained in Supplementary Table 7. c, Measured guarded ramp–value combinations and the unconstrained result. Connecting lines join tested cases and do not represent an optimal frontier. d, Changes in guarded policy cost and in the earliest-service reference cost relative to the unconstrained account. Import caps and upward ramps are enforced using current physical observations; deferred work is never discarded.')
    para(doc,f'Guarded electrical cases retain up to {guard.closing_max.max():.3f} IT MWh of year-end work with deadlines extending beyond the calendar. These obligations remain valued in policy and reference accounts. The zero-lateness result is distinct from zero closing work; the unconstrained primary tests have both.')
    heading(doc,'Financial differences persist beyond the leading event')
    para(doc,f'The 95th percentile of the full-2024 daily settlement-spread range is {event["threshold"]:.4f} US$/MWh. This price-only threshold labels {event["extreme_days"]} of 365 dates in 2025. Those dates contribute US${usd(event["extreme_usd"])} to the WorldMoE-minus-RASC-ME difference; ordinary dates contribute US${usd(event["ordinary_usd"])}. Of the extreme dates, {event["extreme_positive_days"]} have positive differences, and their median difference is US${usd(event["extreme_median_usd"],2)} (Fig. 6).')
    para(doc,f'The largest single-date contribution occurs on {event["top_date"]}: US${usd(event["top_date_usd"])} ({100*event["top_date_share"]:.2f}% of the annual difference). Excluding this date only from financial aggregation leaves US${usd(event["without_top_total_usd"])} overall and US${usd(event["without_top_extreme_usd"])} across remaining extreme dates. Excluding the largest contributing seven-day window, {event["top_week_start"]}–{event["top_week_end"]}, leaves US${usd(event["without_top_week_total_usd"])}. Both exclusions retain a positive annual difference in this observed calendar. Every physical trajectory remains continuous; these outcome-identified exclusions diagnose influence and do not replace the full-year estimate.')
    para(doc,'The early-pathway contrast and the external-pipeline contrast need not share the same concentration. We retain all 90th, 95th and 97.5th percentile labels under three price-state definitions, together with 3, 7, 14 and 28 day bootstrap blocks. These diagnostics describe where observed financial differences arise and how sensitive their uncertainty is to temporal dependence. They do not establish uniform superiority in all extreme market states or a causal response to system scarcity (Supplementary Tables 8–10).')
    figure(doc,'Fig6_event_influence','Fig. 6 | Event dependence and robustness of the final WorldMoE comparisons. a, Daily WorldMoE-minus-RASC-ME differences against the daily across-zone settlement-spread range; the vertical threshold is derived from 2024. b, Absolute extreme- and ordinary-date contributions to the WorldMoE-minus-RASC-ME annual difference; labels also give shares of that observed difference. c, Full-calendar differences and financial totals excluding the largest contributing date or seven-day window of this contrast. d, Paired 95% intervals for the aggregate extreme-date increment using four block lengths and 2,000 resamples each. Exclusions are identified from observed influence; workloads and continuous schedules are unchanged.')
    heading(doc,'Discussion',1)
    para(doc,f'WorldMoE connects predictive information to an explicit service and electricity account. The final decision module saves {100*mean/ref:.2f}% of earliest-service cost in the evaluated four-zone year and completes the 24 h divisible-work contract across all three optimization seeds. A certified savings ceiling places the result on an interpretable scale: the measured capture is {capture:.2f}%, leaving bounded economic headroom while avoiding an unsupported claim of exact optimality.')
    para(doc,'The architectural contribution is the integration of protected price prediction, temporal market–physics processing, workload queries and a differentiable state-cost allocation surrogate within one trainable decision module. Task-oriented optimization and differentiable convex layers provide established methodological foundations.[[12,13,28]] The Gaussian partial moments and constrained QP are not claimed as new mathematical tools. Their role here is to make the forecast-to-cost interface trainable while keeping physical execution and financial settlement independently inspectable.')
    para(doc,'The additional evidence changes what can be inferred from a favorable annual bill. Value certificates separate remaining economic opportunity from observed policy performance. Publication interventions measure an operational information path, including its physical-cost context. Electrical stress tests expose the difference between limiting power and completing the deferred service. Event diagnostics identify the extent to which the financial comparison rests on particular dates. These quantities support different claims and should not be collapsed into a single model-quality score.')
    para(doc,'The evidence is limited to a trace-driven, divisible-work portfolio and one held-out market year for the final decision module. External baselines compare complete pipelines with their retained calibration and control rules. Three optimization seeds share the same pretrained initialization and cached backbone representations. The 2025 calendar is excluded from parameter fitting and per-seed checkpoint selection, but development included inspections of that calendar; the evaluation is not a never-inspected, preregistered blind test. Broader markets, independently trained backbones and a fresh future evaluation period would be needed for stronger generalization claims.')
    para(doc,'The electrical experiments also retain a deliberate boundary: the constrained executor projects frozen requests rather than jointly retraining a controller for each grid restriction. The known-work deadline guard cannot certify unknown future arrivals. The physical forcing is a documented surrogate and the workload is mapped from historical traces; observed facility service-level revenue, endogenous market impact and contractual penalty functions are outside the present electricity account. The numerical result is therefore an operating-value estimate under explicit service, information and physical assumptions.')
    heading(doc,'Methods',1)
    write_methods(doc)
    heading(doc,'Acknowledgements',1)
    para(doc,'We acknowledge the providers of the Eagle traces, NYISO market archives and NASA POWER data. Frontier trace inputs used in the value-bound archive were provided by Oak Ridge National Laboratory; the original dataset attribution and licence remain in the source corpus. The Frontier dataset was produced under UT-Battelle, LLC’s Prime Contract DE-AC05-00OR22725 with the US Department of Energy, with support to the dataset creators from the e-INFRA CZ project (ID: 90254).')
    heading(doc,'Data availability',1)
    para(doc,'The cited NYISO, Eagle, NASA POWER and Frontier providers supply the underlying data. The accompanying source-data package contains the complete final per-seed outcomes, continuous daily financial series, frozen-checkpoint information interventions, all electrical cases, value certificates, price-state sensitivities, forecast scores and figure inputs. Provenance records identify source files and full checkpoint hashes.')
    heading(doc,'Code availability',1)
    para(doc,'The reproducibility package includes the serial training, checkpoint-freezing, continuous replay, extension-analysis, aggregation, plotting and document-authoring scripts. The retained model archive supplies the frozen WorldMoE/R2 feature cache and selected checkpoint lineage. Capacity, work conservation and financial settlement are implemented independently of learned slot values. The internal run identifiers are resolved in the provenance manifest.')
    heading(doc,'References',1)
    for i,key in enumerate(USED,1):para(doc,f'{i}. {REFERENCES[key]}')
    path=OUT/'WorldMoE_Revised_Manuscript_20260928.docx';doc.save(path)
    return doc,path,abstract

def write_methods(doc):
    heading(doc,'Data, workload mapping and chronology',3)
    para(doc,'The evaluation spans 1 January 2025 to 1 January 2026, both at 00:00 America/New_York, preserving daylight-saving transitions. Official day-ahead and time-weighted integrated hourly real-time prices are applied to their four actual quarter-hours.[[15]] The retained archive vintage includes retrospective price corrections. NASA POWER inputs and the archived physical surrogate determine PV and PUE.[[16]] PUE ranges from 1.023698 to 1.092948. Sites have 1.639059 MW variable IT capacity, 0.230700 MW idle demand, 1 MW PV and a 2.150223 MW import limit each; exports are zero. These are modeled portfolio parameters, not measured capacities at the price-settlement locations.')
    para(doc,'Eagle submitted requested nodes are aggregated into quarter-hours and transformed by a training-only quantile bridge without upper-tail capping. Target UTC dates select source dates by their offset from 1 January 2022 modulo the ordered 1,086-date list for November 2018–November 2021. The resulting demand is divisible and spatially normalized. A release at interval a with a 24 h window has deadline a+95, including its release slot. The final controller shortens the planning horizon at the calendar boundary and drains known work in the final hour; it never accesses future arrivals. Outstanding work remains valued if any survives year end. Separate bounds use the archived Frontier submission-resource mapping, without new nonpreemptive-job experiments.[[24]]')
    para(doc,'Learnable price and decision parameters use 2021–2023; physical decision labels are available for 2022–2023. The 2024 year supplies checkpoint, batch and hyperparameter selection without gradient updates for the final module. The frozen WorldMoE lineage is seed 20240829, W0 epoch 80 and W1 epoch 80, selected by the retained 2024 proper-score criterion. W0/W1 are successive phases of one model, and all 59,156,961 backbone parameters remain frozen. Final refinement seeds are 20260920, 20260921 and 20260922. They share the same trained market feature cache and pretrained decision initialization. Each seed is frozen after its own 2024 selection and evaluated on 2025; no 2025 label enters fitting or per-seed checkpoint choice. Earlier inspections of the 2025 calendar are disclosed as a development limitation.')
    heading(doc,'Information and physical observation gates',3)
    para(doc,'Market forecasts issue at 04:55 on the preceding local day, ahead of the normal 05:00 DA submission deadline, using completed information through the preceding midnight.[[17]] Target-day DA is made available at the registered 11:00 publication gate. Market features include zone, target calendar, same-local-hour price lags of 2, 3, 7, 14 and 28 days and trailing 7/28-day summaries. Unknown or unissued targets use fixed training medians. Missing WorldMoE context is zero-filled with an availability indicator; every physical day remains in evaluation.')
    para(doc,'The decision state uses at least a one-quarter-hour lag for observed PV and PUE. A training-only seasonal PV envelope and lagged clearness state form the physical fallback, with a six-hour forecast decay. Current PV scenarios use clock-specific residual quantiles fitted on 2022–2023. PUE uses the last completed observation with an eight-hour decaying prior correction. Realized current physical forcing closes execution balance and settlement, while hypothetical future plans use only issued predictions and causal observations. Independent future spot-price shocks are never revealed to the policy.')
    heading(doc,'Trainable WorldMoE decision architecture',3)
    para(doc,'WorldMoE denotes the forecast-to-execution framework built around the frozen world-model backbone, market representation and trainable decision module. The frozen market head projects intermediate interaction blocks 3, 6 and 10, site summaries, expert states and global/tail context into width-128 tokens, combined with a width-64 daily-profile query and a numerical encoder. The decision module has 227,527 trainable parameters. A 128→64→128 residual adapter modifies frozen R2 features, followed by a market-only scalar price-shift head bounded to ±8 US$/MWh. Market and physical encoders map 135 and 13 inputs to width 64. Bidirectional four-head attention fuses their 24 hourly tokens, each pooled from four quarter-hours; two temporal attention layers have width 64 and feed-forward width 128. Nine work-state features form 96 workload queries. Four-head work-to-time attention and a 192→64→128 refinement layer combine known obligations with temporal evidence. A state encoder and pooled decoder produce PV location/spread, bounded cost bias and an opportunity score. Supplementary Table 11 lists the structural dimensions.')
    para(doc,'Anchored price-identity coupling (APIC) preserves the identity Δp=A−r, where A is DA and r is RT. Before publication, coupled draws define r=A−Δp; after publication, the observed DA anchor gives RT quantiles by reversing settlement-spread quantile indices.[[20,21]] The new market-only shift δ preserves ordered price quantiles and the identity:')
    equation(doc,["r′ = r + δ,     Δp′ = Δp − δ,     A′ = A,     A′ − Δp′ = r′"],1)
    para(doc,'This price branch is computed before queue-conditioned attention. A separate task-only price-equivalent bias is bounded to ±5 US$/MWh. The opportunity score is derived from a ±5 head and centered across time; centering can expand its individual-slot range. Neither quantity is reported as an electricity-price forecast. A bounded cross-moment head links the price and PV cost representation through an affine conditional-price approximation; it is not a fully calibrated joint copula.')
    heading(doc,'Continuous physical costs and differentiable allocation',3)
    para(doc,'For service x, demand L=PUE(Pidle+kx), PV P=max(0,μ+σZ) and imports g=(L−P)+, the decoder uses Gaussian partial moments. With zL=(L−μ)/σ and z0=−μ/σ, let m1(L)=μ[Φ(zL)−Φ(z0)]+σ[φ(z0)−φ(zL)], where Φ and φ are the standard-normal CDF and density. Then')
    equation(doc,["E[(L − P)₊] = L Φ(",('sub','z','L'),") − ",('sub','m','1'),"(L)"],2)
    para(doc,'The corresponding second partial moment supplies E[P(L−P)+]. An affine conditional-price term therefore evaluates expected price times imports without replacing it by the product of marginal means. PV curtailment value and import-deviation fees are included in planning costs. Nine uniform capacity nodes and seven PV-derived knots form differentiable training segments. Deployment additionally includes the reference-service knot and integrates segment endpoints analytically. Negative-price nonconvex pieces use a convex planning proxy; its cost is not a certified bound and never replaces realized settlement.')
    para(doc,'For each arriving cohort, segment widths u and price-equivalent scores c define a box-constrained allocation with a fixed work total a. With xs the service at slot s, the training allocation solves')
    equation(doc,[('sub','min','z'),"  cᵀz + (τ/2)∑ₛ xₛ²,     0 ≤ z ≤ u,     1ᵀz = a"],3)
    para(doc,'The implementation uses τ=2 and a sorted-breakpoint solution for the equality multiplier. Prefix offsets preserve the order of convex slot segments; active-set implicit derivatives propagate through scores, segment capacities and admitted work. The layer follows established differentiable optimization principles.[[28]] It is an exact solution of this surrogate QP, apart from numerical precision and nondifferentiable active-set boundaries; it is not an exact solution of the full annual scheduling problem.')
    para(doc,'Training unrolls 16 consecutively arriving cohorts through a conserved reservation state over 111 quarters. Of 2,376 training episodes, 27 are excluded by a sufficient common-window capacity test, leaving 2,349; 320 separate 2024 snapshots support surrogate validation. Evaluation workloads are never filtered. The loss combines separately scored price quantiles, PV pinball loss, marginal-cost regression, regularization and realized episode cost relative to earliest service:')
    equation(doc,["ℒ = ",('sub','ℒ','price')," + E[D/Z] + 2",('sub','ℒ','PV')," + 0.2",('sub','ℒ','cost')," + ℛ"],4)
    para(doc,'D is the realized episode dollar difference, and Z is a training-only per-episode normalization equal to released-work reference electricity energy times US$50/MWh, with a numerical floor. Thus the training objective averages normalized episode costs; it is not the annual aggregate-dollar objective. The price loss weights DA, settlement-spread and RT pinball terms by 0.5, 1 and 0.5 after training-only scale normalization.[[18,19]] The PV term uses seven quantiles, and marginal cost uses a Huber loss. Opportunity, cross-moment and price-shift terms are regularized. Realized future outcomes supply training targets only.')
    heading(doc,'Optimization and economic early stopping',3)
    para(doc,'The refinement uses AdamW with learning rate 10⁻⁴, weight decay 10⁻⁴, batch size 32 and gradient-norm clipping at 1. BF16 training reuses cached frozen features; physical cost outputs and validation calculations retain their required precision. The maximum is 12 epochs, with full-2024 economic evaluation every two epochs. A checkpoint must have late and closing work below 10⁻⁸ IT MWh and a normalized price score no more than 2% above the fixed reference 0.4193302095. After at least six epochs, three economic evaluations without a gain exceeding US$50 trigger stopping. The actual highest eligible saving is restored, even when its improvement is smaller than the stopping min-delta. No 2025 result changes this choice.')
    para(doc,'The deployment controller recomputes hypothetical service for already released work but executes only the current action. Known deadline prefixes constrain the minimum current service; future capacity uses a 0.9 safety fraction. The future objective is expected continuation cost (scenario-recourse mixing coefficient zero). Current actions are searched on a 0.025-work-unit grid plus physical and reference knots. Only 16-cohort training allocations are differentiated; the full NumPy/Numba annual current-action replay is not backpropagated. Its independently settled 2024 result is the economic selection criterion.')
    heading(doc,'Settlement, references and external baselines',3)
    para(doc,'At each site and interval, PUE multiplies idle plus variable IT demand, PV is used up to load, and imports close the balance. The shared DA position is fixed across policies. DA terms cancel in paired saving, but its common US$2,409.78 component is retained in annual costs. Transaction friction charges US$1/MWh on absolute paired import deviations; PV curtailment carries US$1/MWh. Closing work is valued at fixed pre-2024 zone-median RT prices, maximum registered PUE and the IT conversion. No learned terminal value or assumed lateness penalty changes the reported account.')
    para(doc,'The earliest-service reference completes released work as capacity permits. FIFO assignment under the common deadline measures late work once and retains it until completion. During electrically constrained tests, both policy requests and earliest service pass through the same limit. The account compares costs, not gross exposure payoffs. Shifted IT is positive reference-minus-policy service energy, and recovery is the opposite import deviation.')
    para(doc,'Frozen external baselines include RASC-ME, six statistical price pipelines, the price-only neural head, RASC direct, published-DA persistence and myopic control, a causal 24-hour RT-lag control, and DAC. Statistical models use the retained seasonal, ridge, LightGBM and calibration configurations.[[22,23]] RASC and comparator calibration retain their documented 2024 calibration split; no comparator is refitted in this revision. This differs from the final WorldMoE module, for which 2024 performs selection only. Common forcing, demand, capacities and accounting make the total operating outcomes comparable while leaving the pipelines’ own calibration and controllers intact.')
    para(doc,'RASC-ME is the retained regime-adaptive settlement-calibration pipeline with reservation-state physical execution. Its LightGBM quantile models use 300 trees, learning rate 0.04, 15 leaves, minimum leaf size 100, L2 penalty 10, 127 bins and seed 20260917. A mixture of full 2021–2023 and 2023-only models is selected over weights 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 and 1; the retained full-history weight is 1. Residual calibration groups by zone, volatility regime and six-hour block and shrinks towards global quantile corrections. Seasonal-ME uses zone–hour spread quantiles, Ridge-ME uses penalty 100 and training residual quantiles, and the LightGBM variants isolate recent history, daily-state inputs and global residual calibration. Published-DA and lagged-RT controls use their named information signals, while DAC retains its fixed one-hour internal control clock.')
    heading(doc,'Certified savings ceilings',3)
    para(doc,'The bound grants perfect price and physical information and relaxes implementability while retaining released work, site capacity, in-calendar deadline prefixes, common DA terms, friction and closing-work valuation. Convexification enlarges the feasible set in nonconvex negative-price regimes. For a linear relaxation min c0+cᵀx with Ax=b and 0≤x≤u, any multiplier λ supplies the box-minimized lower bound[[27]]')
    equation(doc,["L(λ) = ",('sub','c','0')," + λᵀb + ∑",('sub','u','i')," min{0, ",('sub','c','i')," − (Aᵀλ)ᵢ}"],5)
    para(doc,'Each site certificate is conservatively rounded down and summed. Reference cost minus this lower cost bound is an upper bound on savings. The reported capture fraction divides realized savings by that upper bound. Bound construction uses realized future information only in the explicitly labeled certificate calculation; no certificate trajectory or future outcome enters the executable controller.')
    heading(doc,'Information intervention and electrical execution guard',3)
    para(doc,'The information intervention applies the identical final checkpoint and decision rule while suppressing target availability until DA publication. It masks associated neural market/state-cost access and issued physical priors; completed observations, seasonal fallback and the post-publication branch remain. All three seeds are replayed across the same full year. No parameter or controller setting is reselected for this intervention.')
    para(doc,'Electrical stress tests constrain upward import ramps only. The two ramp increments are 0.5375556 and 0.2687778 MW per site per quarter-hour. A causal projector tracks cumulative released, requested and served work. At each time it reads current PV/PUE to enforce the import/ramp capacity and carries unmet requests forward. For arrived unfinished work B_t(u) due by u, the deadline guard requires')
    equation(doc,[('sub','r','t')," = ",('sub','max','u ≥ t')," [",('sub','B','t'),"(u) − (u − t)",('sub','K','min'),"]₊"],6)
    para(doc,'Kmin uses zero PV, maximum registered PUE and the smaller of the import cap and upward-ramp increment; without a ramp limit it uses the cap alone. Actual service is the minimum of current physical capacity, released unfinished work and the larger of accumulated requested service and rt. This protects known deadline prefixes conservatively. It does not promise feasibility for unknown future arrivals. Thirty policy-condition cases cover two execution rules, five electrical restrictions and three final seeds.')
    heading(doc,'Financial uncertainty and event influence',3)
    para(doc,'Daily service-adjusted savings subtract changes in outstanding-work value from daily cash savings, preserving the full-year total. CVaR95 averages the worst 5% probability mass of daily losses, including a fractional boundary day.[[25]] Paired intervals use 2,000 circular blocks of seven days with random seed 20260928, applied to daily differences averaged across the three refinement seeds.[[26]] These intervals describe dependent financial variation within the observed calendar, not uncertainty across independent market years. Resampled financial sums are not new feasible dispatch trajectories and are not compared with the fixed-calendar optimization certificate.')
    para(doc,'The primary event label is daily max(DA−RT)−min(DA−RT) across sites and actual intervals, thresholded at the 2024 95th percentile. Sensitivities retain 90th and 97.5th percentiles and definitions based on maximum absolute spread and maximum RT price. All leave-one-date and contiguous seven-day financial exclusions are computed without changing continuous physical trajectories. The largest contributors are identified after observation and reported as influence diagnostics. Block lengths of 3, 7, 14 and 28 days are retained without choosing a favorable uncertainty estimate.')
    heading(doc,'Computation and reproducibility',3)
    para(doc,'Experiments use Python 3.13, PyTorch 2.9 and an NVIDIA RTX 3080. The final refinement seeds run sequentially. Frozen feature caching, hourly context tokens and BF16 training reduce repeated computation; early stopping uses actual annual economics. Checkpoint hashes, selection records, state and forecast sources, per-seed daily accounts and all figure source rows accompany the code. Numerical feasibility checks use a 10⁻⁸ IT MWh reporting tolerance for late and closing work; substantive violations are retained rather than removed.')

def write_supplement():
    doc=docnew();para(doc,'Supplementary Information','Title');para(doc,TITLE)
    heading(doc,'Scope and model lineage',1)
    para(doc,'This supplement reports the final WorldMoE decision module and the value-bound, information-timing, electrical-delivery and event-influence analyses. External baselines are complete frozen operating pipelines. All added information and electrical interventions use the selected final checkpoints. Parameter fitting uses 2021–2023, physical labels use 2022–2023, and the 2024 year selects the final module without weight updates. The 2025 calendar supplies temporal evaluation and is excluded from parameter fitting and per-seed checkpoint selection; earlier development inspections limit a strict blind-test interpretation.')
    para(doc,'The three refinement runs share the same pretrained decision initialization and frozen WorldMoE/R2 feature cache. W0 and W1 are successive frozen backbone phases, not separate newly trained models. Seeds change batch order and auxiliary price-target sampling. The initial checkpoint is eligible for restoration if later refinement does not improve eligible 2024 annual savings. A seed is retained irrespective of whether its 2025 result reaches the descriptive 80% savings-ceiling target.')
    rows=[]
    for r in F.itertuples():rows.append([str(r.seed),str(r.completed_epochs),str(r.selected_epoch),usd(r.selection_savings_usd,2),usd(r.saving_usd,2),perc(100*r.capture_lower_fraction),f'{r.late_IT_mwh:.3g}',usd(r.closing_it_mwh,3)])
    table(doc,'Supplementary Table 1 | Final optimization seeds and complete-year service outcomes.', ['Seed','Epochs\nrun','Epoch\nselected','2024 saving\nUS$','2025 saving\nUS$','Ceiling\n%','Late IT\nMWh','Closing IT\nMWh'],rows,[.86,.47,.51,1.08,1.08,.65,.80,.65])
    rows=[[str(r.seed),usd(r.post_rt_pinball_usd_mwh,4),usd(r.post_rt_median_mae_usd_mwh,4),perc(r.post_rt_coverage90_pct),usd(r.post_rt_interval_width_usd_mwh,3)] for r in FS.itertuples()]
    table(doc,'Supplementary Table 2 | Final queue-independent post-publication RT forecast scores. These exclude task-only cost and opportunity adjustments.', ['Seed','Pinball\nUS$/MWh','Median MAE\nUS$/MWh','90% coverage\n%','Interval width\nUS$/MWh'],rows,[1.05,1.45,1.45,1.45,1.45])
    heading(doc,'Certified attainable value',1)
    para(doc,'The bounds use an expanded feasible set with perfect information. They are independent of trained forecasts, checkpoint choices and current-action search. The dual certificate remains valid for any multiplier because the remaining box minimization is evaluated explicitly. Site-level downward rounding protects the reported lower cost bound from floating-point overstatement. Closing-work valuation is kept in the relaxation, so a policy with zero closing work can still be compared with the same valid ceiling. A lower bound on cost is not a feasible schedule and need not equal the true minimum implementable cost.')
    rows=[[str(int(r.year)),r.trace,str(int(r.window_h)),usd(r.reference_cost_usd,2),usd(r.lower_bound_usd,2),usd(r.maximum_saving_upper_usd,2)] for r in bound.itertuples()]
    table(doc,'Supplementary Table 3 | Twelve workload–year–window cost certificates. Values are US dollars; no learned policy result is substituted for a bound.', ['Year','Trace','Window\nh','Reference cost','Cost lower bound','Savings ceiling'],rows,[.58,.85,.55,1.57,1.57,1.57])
    heading(doc,'Publication information and paired uncertainty',1)
    para(doc,'The intervention changes only the native early information path for the final checkpoints: target availability, the associated neural state-cost path, and issued physical-prior access are masked before DA publication. Current completed observations and seasonal fallbacks are preserved. This is a joint pathway intervention, not a pure price-feature ablation. The full and restricted runs each include every 2025 interval. Their daily service-adjusted difference is paired by local date before block resampling.')
    table(doc,'Supplementary Table 4 | Same-checkpoint information intervention for all final seeds. Service residuals refer to the restricted runs.', ['Seed','Epoch','Native saving\nUS$','Restricted saving\nUS$','Early-path increment\nUS$','Late IT\nMWh','Closing IT\nMWh'],[[str(r.seed),str(r.epoch),usd(r.native_usd,2),usd(r.post_only_usd,2),usd(r.early_path_usd,2),f'{r.restricted_late_it_mwh:.3g}',f'{r.restricted_closing_it_mwh:.3g}'] for r in I.itertuples()],[.85,.4,1.2,1.2,1.25,1.05,1.05])
    rows=[[public(key),usd(r.difference_usd,2),usd(r.lower95_usd,2),usd(r.upper95_usd,2)] for key,r in P.iterrows()]
    table(doc,'Supplementary Table 5 | Paired seven-day-block financial intervals. The 2,000 resamples use seed-mean daily differences and random seed 20260928.', ['Contrast','Annual difference\nUS$','Lower 95%\nUS$','Upper 95%\nUS$'],rows,[2.65,1.45,1.45,1.45])
    heading(doc,'Electrical execution and preserved service debt',1)
    para(doc,'A projected request can be delayed by a physical constraint even when its unconstrained plan was deadline-feasible. The executor retains this service debt and cannot hide it by changing the released workload or deleting a missed request. The deadline guard can serve already arrived work earlier than the request stream when its prefix bound requires it. Because its conservative future capacity can be much lower than realized PV-supported capacity, value loss is not an estimate of the optimal economic price of the electrical constraint. References are separately projected through each identical condition.')
    rows=[]
    for r in G.itertuples():rows.append(['Guarded' if r.experiment=='CdfI3G' else 'Projection',r.scenario,usd(r.saving_usd,2),usd(r.policy_cost_usd,2),usd(r.reference_cost_usd,2),perc(r.savings_retained_pct)])
    table(doc,'Supplementary Table 6 | Electrical economics. Each row averages three final seeds; references match the electrical condition.', ['Execution','Condition','Saving\nUS$','Policy cost\nUS$','Reference cost\nUS$','Retained\n%'],rows,[.83,1.05,1.27,1.35,1.35,.75])
    rows=[]
    for r in G.itertuples():rows.append(['Guarded' if r.experiment=='CdfI3G' else 'Projection',r.scenario,f'{r.late_max:.4g}',f'{r.closing_max:.4g}',f'{r.upward_ramp_mw:.6f}',f'{r.cap_excess_mw:.2g}',f'{r.ramp_excess_mw:.2g}'])
    table(doc,'Supplementary Table 7 | Electrical and service limits. Maxima are taken across the three seeds; numerical residuals remain visible.', ['Execution','Condition','Max late\nIT MWh','Max closing\nIT MWh','Upward ramp\nMW / 15 min','Cap excess\nMW','Ramp excess\nMW'],rows,[.82,1.03,.85,.85,1.15,1.05,1.05])
    heading(doc,'Event definitions and influence',1)
    para(doc,'Price-state labels are descriptive properties of the evaluated calendar. The three definitions are the daily across-zone spread range, maximum absolute spread and maximum RT price. All thresholds are estimated from 2024 alone. A positive aggregate extreme-date difference does not imply that most extreme dates favor the model, and it does not establish a causal response to grid scarcity. Financial exclusions are chosen by observed contribution and leave every original physical schedule intact.')
    ev=pd.read_csv(D/'worldmoe_event_sensitivity.csv');ev=ev[ev.contrast.isin(['Joint WorldMoE vs original RASC-ME','Joint WorldMoE early information pathway'])]
    rows=[]
    for r in ev.itertuples():rows.append(['vs RASC-ME' if 'RASC' in r.contrast else 'Early path',r.definition,perc(100*r.quantile,1),str(r.extreme_days),usd(r.extreme_usd,1),usd(r.ordinary_usd,1),usd(r.extreme_lower95,1),usd(r.extreme_upper95,1)])
    table(doc,'Supplementary Table 8 | Event-definition and threshold sensitivity. All monetary values are US dollars; intervals use seven-day blocks.', ['Contrast','Definition','q (%)','Extreme\ndates','Extreme\nUS$','Ordinary\nUS$','Lower 95%','Upper 95%'],rows,[.73,1.18,.48,.68,.98,.98,.98,.98])
    blocks=pd.read_csv(D/'worldmoe_event_block_intervals.csv');blocks=blocks[blocks.contrast.isin(['Joint WorldMoE vs original RASC-ME','Joint WorldMoE early information pathway'])]
    rows=[['vs RASC-ME' if 'RASC' in r.contrast else 'Early path',str(r.block_days),usd(r.extreme_usd,2),usd(r.lower95_usd,2),usd(r.upper95_usd,2)] for r in blocks.itertuples()]
    table(doc,'Supplementary Table 9 | Block-length sensitivity for the aggregate extreme-date increment.', ['Contrast','Block\ndays','Extreme increment\nUS$','Lower 95%\nUS$','Upper 95%\nUS$'],rows,[1.45,.6,1.7,1.6,1.6])
    rows=[]
    for key in ['Joint WorldMoE vs original RASC-ME','Joint WorldMoE early information pathway']:
        e=E[key];label='vs RASC-ME' if 'RASC' in key else 'Early path'
        rows += [[label,'None',usd(e['total_usd'],2),usd(e['extreme_usd'],2)],
                 [label,e['top_date'],usd(e['without_top_total_usd'],2),usd(e['without_top_extreme_usd'],2)],
                 [label,e['top_week_start']+' to '+e['top_week_end'],usd(e['without_top_week_total_usd'],2),'See source rows']]
    table(doc,'Supplementary Table 10 | Outcome-identified exclusions. Each contrast has its own largest contributing date/week here; Fig. 6c shows the WorldMoE-minus-RASC-ME contrast.', ['Contrast','Excluded local dates','Retained total\nUS$','Retained extreme\nUS$'],rows,[1.2,2.55,1.65,1.6])
    heading(doc,'Architecture, loss and replication details',1)
